.TH "sudoconsole" "1" "Aug 2026" "sudoconsole " "sudoconsole Manual"

.SH NAME
sudoconsole-exec - Run a command under sudo, applying the policy


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsudoconsole exec [--]  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
exec runs the supplied command under sudo. The command line is
evaluated against the active policy; on Block the command does not run
unless --policy-override  is supplied. When --policy-override is
used, sudoconsole prompts for confirmation (skippable with --yes) and
records the reason in the audit log. The cache is consulted first; if
expired, exec either fails fast (no secret supplied) or silently
refreshes via the password supplied via $SUDOCONSOLE_PASSWORD.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for exec

.PP
\fB--policy-override\fP=""
	bypass a Block decision and proceed anyway; the value is recorded in the audit log as the override reason (interactive confirm required unless --yes)

.PP
\fB--user\fP=""
	sudo --user override (advanced)

.PP
\fB--yes\fP[=false]
	skip the interactive confirmation prompt


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--cache-timeout\fP=0
	sudo cache timeout in seconds (60-3600); overrides config file

.PP
\fB--config\fP=""
	path to the TOML config file (default: platform XDG/Application Support location)

.PP
\fB--format\fP=""
	output format: human or json (overrides config file)

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP=""
	log level: silent, error, warn, info, debug (overrides config file)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  sudoconsole exec apt update
  sudoconsole exec --policy-override "debugging ssh tunnel" systemctl restart nginx
  sudoconsole --format json exec apt update
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBsudoconsole(1)\fP


